// cookiewrite.nv — a `Set-Cookie` header written, with the rules
// enforced rather than documented.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// EVERY WRITER HERE ANSWERS A `Result`, and that is the package's main
// argument.  A cookie library whose `to_string` cannot fail is one that
// happily emits `Set-Cookie: __Host-sid=x; Domain=example.com`, which
// every browser silently drops.  The server then sees a request with no
// session, concludes the user is logged out, and the failure is three
// layers away from the line that caused it.  The check costs four
// comparisons, and `cookieattr.check_attrs` is where they live.
//
// A CALLER WHO IS SURE CAN STILL WRITE ANYTHING, with
// `set_cookie_unchecked` — because a test that wants to produce a
// malformed header, and a proxy that is passing one through, both
// exist.  The name says what it skipped.
//
// WRITING INTO A CALLER'S BUFFER IS THE OTHER FORM.  A response is
// assembled into one buffer and a header that allocated a string per
// cookie would allocate one per cookie per response.  `set_cookie_into`
// appends and answers the byte count, which is the shape every writer
// on the grid has.

use civil
use cookieattr

// Write a `Set-Cookie` header value — everything after `Set-Cookie: `.
//
// The value and not the whole line, because a server holds its headers
// as name/value pairs and a writer that produced `Set-Cookie: …` would
// have to be sliced apart again.
//
// ```novo
// use cookieattr
// use cookiewrite
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match cookiewrite.set_cookie("sid", "abc", cookieattr.defaults())
//         Ok(h)  => println(h)
//         Err(_) => println("refused")
// ```
pub fn set_cookie(name: Str, value: Str,
                  a: cookieattr.CookieAttrs) -> Result<Str, cookieattr.CookieError> []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.set_cookie")

// Write a `Set-Cookie` value without checking anything.
//
// For a proxy passing a header through, and for a test that wants to
// produce one a browser will drop. The name is the disclosure.
pub fn set_cookie_unchecked(name: Str, value: Str, a: cookieattr.CookieAttrs) -> Str []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.set_cookie_unchecked")

// Append a `Set-Cookie` value to a buffer the caller owns.
//
// Answers the number of bytes appended, or the refusal. The
// allocation-free form, for a server assembling a response into one
// buffer.
pub fn set_cookie_into(name: Str, value: Str, a: cookieattr.CookieAttrs,
                       mut dst: Cursor) -> Result<Int, cookieattr.CookieError> []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.set_cookie_into")

// How many bytes `set_cookie` would produce.
//
// So a caller can size a buffer before writing into one, which is the
// pair `set_cookie_into` needs to be usable.
pub fn set_cookie_len(name: Str, value: Str, a: cookieattr.CookieAttrs) -> Int []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.set_cookie_len")

// The header that DELETES a cookie.
//
// Deleting a cookie is setting it to an empty value with an expiry in
// the past, and the attributes that matter are the ones that must
// MATCH the cookie being deleted: a cookie set with `Domain` and
// `Path=/app` is not deleted by a header with neither, because the
// browser keys the cookie on that triple and the deletion lands on a
// different cookie. That rule is why this is a function rather than a
// paragraph in a README: the caller passes the attributes it originally
// set, and this fills in the expiry.
//
// ```novo
// use cookieattr
// use cookiewrite
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match cookiewrite.delete_cookie("sid", cookieattr.defaults())
//         Ok(h)  => println(h)
//         Err(_) => println("refused")
// ```
pub fn delete_cookie(name: Str, a: cookieattr.CookieAttrs) -> Result<Str, cookieattr.CookieError> []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.delete_cookie")

// Format an `Expires` value the way RFC 6265 § 4.1.1 requires.
//
// One spelling on the way out, however many on the way in: the RFC says
// a server MUST use the RFC 1123 form — `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT`
// — and the tolerance in `cookieparse.parse_expires` is for reading
// other people's headers, not for writing our own.
pub fn format_expires(at: civil.CivilDateTime) -> Str []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.format_expires")

// Write a `Cookie` REQUEST header value from a list of pairs.
//
// The client direction: `name=value; name=value`, no attributes,
// because a client never tells a server what it knows about a cookie.
// Here rather than left to the caller because a client that built this
// header with `str.join` would get the separator right and the byte
// rules wrong.
pub fn cookie_header(pairs: [(Str, Str)]) -> Result<Str, cookieattr.CookieError> []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.cookie_header")

// A cookie value with the quoting RFC 6265 § 4.1.1 allows, if it needs
// any.
//
// Quotes are added only when the value contains a byte the bare grammar
// refuses AND that quoting makes legal — which is a small set, because
// a control byte is illegal quoted too. A value that cannot be made
// legal is refused rather than quoted into something a browser will cut
// short at the first semicolon.
pub fn quote_value(value: Str) -> Result<Str, cookieattr.CookieError> []
    todo("cookie-nv.cookiewrite.quote_value")
